# Conclusion

The application was vulnerable to an **Insecure Direct Object Reference (IDOR)** that allowed authenticated users to retrieve arbitrary account information by modifying the `id` parameter.

The impact was significantly increased because the administrator's password was exposed within the account response. This enabled full privilege escalation without exploiting authentication mechanisms, ultimately allowing unrestricted administrative access and complete compromise of the application's user management functionality.

---

## Key Takeaways

- Never trust client-supplied object identifiers.
- Always enforce authorization checks at the object level.
- Passwords must never be exposed after registration.
- Sensitive operations must rely exclusively on server-side identity validation.

